    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Oral ursodeoxycholic acid can sometimes dissolve sm, radiolucent (cholest) stones in a functioning, nonobstructed GB; it's taken for many months, for pts who decline surg or are @ ↑surg risk."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Works best on tiny stones (< 0.5 cm); success = much lower for lrger stones, and stones recur in 50% w.in 5 years, so cholecystectomy = preferred unless surg risk = ↑."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "It can help prevent stones in pts w morbid obesity losing wt rapidly after bariatric surg on a very ↓calorie diet, or w GLP-1 receptor agonists."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
